[{"slug":"introduction","href":"/docs/introduction","title":"Introduction","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Quick start","hint":"Overview & getting started","headings":[],"body":"User Documentation Welcome to the end-user documentation of MarkText. Quick start:** Basics Editing in depth Spelling Markdown syntax Further documents:** Frequently asked questions (FAQ) Application data directory Command line interface Environment variables Export a document Image handling Image uploader configuration Installation instructions Key bindings Portable mode Preferences Themes Themes for exporting Interested in developer documentation? Please see here."},{"slug":"installation","href":"/docs/installation","title":"Installation","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Quick start","hint":"Download & install on every platform","headings":[{"id":"windows","text":"Windows","depth":2},{"id":"macos","text":"macOS","depth":2},{"id":"linux","text":"Linux","depth":2},{"id":"verify-the-download","text":"Verify the download","depth":2},{"id":"build-from-source","text":"Build from source","depth":2},{"id":"updating","text":"Updating","depth":2},{"id":"uninstall","text":"Uninstall","depth":2}],"body":"Installation MarkText is a free, open-source Markdown editor for **Linux**, **macOS** and **Windows**. Pre-built binaries are published with every release on GitHub releases. Pick the one that matches your platform. Windows Artifact | When to choose | --- | --- | | Recommended. NSIS installer; per-user install, lets you pick the install directory, creates Start Menu and Desktop shortcuts. | | Portable zip. Extract anywhere and run . See Portable mode for details on keeping your data alongside the app. | After installing, MarkText registers itself as a handler for , , , , and files. macOS Artifact | When to choose | --- | --- | | Apple Silicon (M1 / M2 / M3 / M4). | | Intel Macs. | | Plain zip alternative to the DMG. | Open the DMG and drag MarkText into your **Applications** folder. Builds are not currently notarized, so the first launch may prompt the system Gatekeeper — right-click the app and choose **Open** to accept it once. You can also install via Homebrew Cask: Linux MarkText is shipped in five Linux formats. Most users want the AppImage. Artifact | When to choose | --- | --- | | Recommended. Runs on most distros without root. and double-click (or run directly). | | Debian, Ubuntu, Linux Mint, Pop!_OS, … ( ). | | Fedora, RHEL, openSUSE, … ( ). | | Ubuntu / any snap-enabled distro ( ). | | Portable tarball. Extract and run the included binary. | Arch Linux users can install MarkText from the AUR ( ). See Linux notes for distro-specific tips (sandbox flags, font configuration, file-association quirks). Verify the download Every release contains a file with SHA-512 hashes. To verify: Compare the value to the entry in on the release page. Build from source If you'd rather build from source — for example to track , to run on an architecture we don't publish a binary for, or to contribute — see the Build instructions in the developer docs. A minimal recap: Output installers land in the repository's folder. Updating MarkText checks for updates on launch (this can be disabled under **Preferences → General → Updates**). When an update is published, the app downloads it in the background and installs on next restart. Portable installs and the AppImage do not auto-update — re-download the latest artifact when you want to upgrade. Uninstall Platform | How | --- | --- | Windows | **Settings → Apps**, or run the bundled . | macOS | Drag **MarkText.app** to the Trash. Optionally also remove . | Linux (.deb) | | Linux (.rpm) | | Linux (snap) | | Linux (AppImage / tar.gz) | Delete the file you extracted. | To wipe MarkText's user data as well, remove its application data directory."},{"slug":"basics","href":"/docs/basics","title":"Basics","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Quick start","hint":"The interface, files & tabs","headings":[{"id":"getting-started","text":"Getting started","depth":2},{"id":"interface","text":"Interface","depth":3},{"id":"open-and-modify-markdown-files","text":"Open and modify markdown files","depth":2},{"id":"open-your-first-file","text":"Open your first file","depth":3},{"id":"save-your-edited-file","text":"Save your edited file","depth":3},{"id":"open-a-directory","text":"Open a directory","depth":3},{"id":"themes","text":"Themes","depth":2},{"id":"preferences","text":"Preferences","depth":2}],"body":"Basics Getting started MarkText is a realtime preview editor for markdown with various markdown extensions. You can simply write and edit text and MarkText hides all unnecessary syntax elements. When you first start MarkText an empty editor window is shown. You can see key bindings or command palette ( CmdOrCtrl + Shift + P ) for all available commands or just type to get an overlay with available text elements. MarkText provides a minimal and simple interface and in the next sections you can learn more about the interface and features. Interface Toggle sidebar The sidebar consists of three panels and you can toggle the sidebar by pressing CmdOrCtrl + J : Filesystem explorer (tree view) of the opened root directory. The tree also exposes a collapsible *Opened Files* subsection — toggle it via the preference. Find in files Table of contents of the selected tab Toggle tabs MarkText can be used as a single editor but opens all files in a separate tab. Tabs can be toggled via CmdOrCtrl + Alt + B and reordered by drag and drop. Want to use tabs without showing them?** You can hide tabs and use key bindings such as CmdOrCtrl + Tab to cycle through tabs or the sidebar *opened files* panel. Switch between editor modes You can use CmdOrCtrl + Alt + S to switch between the preview and source-code editor. The realtime preview editor is the default editor with many features. A detailed overview of all features can be found here. Typewriter and focus mode Use CmdOrCtrl + Shift + F to enter distraction free focus mode or CmdOrCtrl + Alt + T for typewriter. :smile_cat:Edit modes:dog: Source Code | Typewriter | Focus | :--------------------:|:------------------------:|:-------------------:| | | | Open and modify markdown files Open your first file You can use the menu or press CmdOrCtrl + O to open a file dialog to choose a markdown file. Another way to is to launch MarkText with directories or files via command line. Save your edited file After some modifications you can save your file via CmdOrCtrl + S or use *save as* to use a different file name. Open a directory MarkText also has support to open a directory via CmdOrCtrl + Shift + O or the sidebar button *Open Folder*. After opening a directory all files and directories are shown in the sidebar tree view. The tree view allows you to open further files, browse and modify files or directories inside the opened root directory. Above the tree view are all opened files located. You can also use quick open ( CmdOrCtrl + P ) to quickly open a file from the opened root directory or editor and navigate via arrow keys or select a file via mouse. To view another sidebar panel like find in files click on the left sidebar icons. Themes You can change the application theme by clicking on an entry under the themes application menu. Preferences You can control and modify all preferences in the settings window or edit in the application data directory. Detailed information about the preference file can be found here. Genera"},{"slug":"editing","href":"/docs/editing","title":"Editing in depth","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Quick start","hint":"Shortcuts, format bar & find/replace","headings":[{"id":"text-manipulation","text":"Text manipulation","depth":2},{"id":"selections","text":"Selections","depth":2},{"id":"deleting","text":"Deleting","depth":2},{"id":"brackets-and-quotes-autocompletion","text":"Brackets and quotes autocompletion","depth":2},{"id":"links","text":"Links","depth":2},{"id":"formatting","text":"Formatting","depth":2},{"id":"editing-features","text":"Editing features","depth":2},{"id":"file-encoding","text":"File encoding","depth":2},{"id":"line-endings","text":"Line endings","depth":2},{"id":"find-and-replace","text":"Find and replace","depth":2}],"body":"Editing in Depth Let us take a look at the realtime editor and editing features. Text manipulation MarkText shows you formatted text in realtime, where you can simply write and edit text, as well as use markdown syntax. To improve your writing efficiency there are a lot of key bindings for better text manipulation. In the preferences you can control the editor settings such as font settings, autocompletion and line width. Selections You can select text with your mouse cursor, double click on a word or use the keyboard Shift + Arrow Keys . Format overlay:** The format overlay is a pop-up that automatically appears when you're selecting text. You can easily transform text and inline markdown, and remove formatting. Bold Italics Underline Strikethrough Inline code Inline math formulas Create link Create image Remove formatting Further overlays are available for emojis, links, images and tables. Deleting Do you want to delete headings, lists or tables? Just select the area and press backspace. Brackets and quotes autocompletion You can configure MarkText to autocomplete markdown syntax, brackets and quotes. By default , , , , , , and are completed when the first character is typed. Links Links are displayed by default as normal text, but if you click on a link it is shown as a markdown link with title and URL, as shown below: Formatting MarkText will automatically format your markdown document according CommonMark and GitHub Flavored Markdown specification. Some settings, such as list indentation, can be controlled via preferences. Editing features Quick insert When you start a new line, just type @ to access a pop-up with all available markdown features. At this point, you can choose an entry and the line is transformed into the selected attribute. Line transformer You can transform a line into another type by clicking on the highlighted icon in the image below and select . Furthermore, you can duplicate the selected line, create a paragraph above the selected line or delete the line. Table tools It's sometimes hard to write and manage tables in markdown. In MarkText you can press CmdOrCtrl + Shift + T to get a table dialog and create a table with variable row and column count. Both row and column count can be changed via the table tools (first icon above the table) later if necessary. You can use all inline styles in a table cell and align the text via table tools at the top of the table. Insert and delete rows and columns:** You can insert or delete a new row or column by clicking on an existing cell and clicking on the menu on the right side for rows or bottom for columns. Move table cells:** You can simply move a row or column by clicking on the cell menu (like above) and dragging it by holding your left mouse button like this: Image tools MarkText provides an image viewer and a pop-up to select and label images. You can resize any image using your mouse cursor; changes are applied in realtime. By clicking on an image or writing a pop-up automat"},{"slug":"spelling","href":"/docs/spelling","title":"Spelling","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Quick start","hint":"Spell checker & dictionaries","headings":[{"id":"features","text":"Features","depth":2},{"id":"manage-dictionaries","text":"Manage dictionaries","depth":2},{"id":"macos-spell-checker","text":"macOS spell checker","depth":3},{"id":"windows-spell-checker","text":"Windows spell checker","depth":3},{"id":"hunspell","text":"Hunspell","depth":3}],"body":"Spelling MarkText can automatically check your text for misspelled words as you type and suggest corrections. You just need to enable spell checking in settings under *spelling* to never miss a misspelled word. We're using Hunspell for Linux and older Windows versions and on macOS and Window 10 you can choose between Hunspell or the system spell checker (default). You can control the default proofing language via settings but can change the language at runtime via right-click menu entry under without changing the default language. By default MarkText only support American English for Hunspell and the local available languages for the system spell checker. You can download 42 languages for Hunspell and many more for macOS and Windows 10 via system settings. Features Automatic language detection:** MarkText can try to automatically detect the language while typing and we're currently support over 160 languages by Compact Language Detector. Don't underline misspelled words:** If you don't like that all spelling mistakes are red highlighted, you can disable this feature in settings but still benefit from manually spell checking via right-click menu. Disabling constantly spell checking will also improve performance overall. Adding words to dictionary:** You can add words to the selected dictionary by right-clicking on a misspelled word and select or remove a previously added word. If you want to temporary ignore a word, select . Manage dictionaries macOS spell checker You need to add the additional language dictionaries via *\"Language & Region\"* in your system preferences pane. Windows spell checker On Windows 10, you need to add additional language dictionaries via *\"Language\"* in your *\"Time & language\"* settings. Add the additional language(s) and download the *\"Basic typing\"* language option for each language. Hunspell Please go to spelling settings and scroll to the bottom. Now you see a list of available language dictionaries and can add additional dictionaries via the drop-down menu at the bottom. Please note that an active internet connection is required to download a dictionary!"},{"slug":"markdown-syntax","href":"/docs/markdown-syntax","title":"Markdown syntax","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Quick start","hint":"Every element MarkText renders","headings":[{"id":"table-of-contents","text":"Table of contents","depth":2},{"id":"headings","text":"Headings","depth":2},{"id":"h2","text":"H2","depth":2},{"id":"h3","text":"H3","depth":3},{"id":"h2-heading","text":"h2 Heading","depth":2},{"id":"h3-heading","text":"h3 Heading","depth":3},{"id":"paragraphs","text":"Paragraphs","depth":2},{"id":"breaks","text":"Breaks","depth":2},{"id":"horizontal-rule","text":"Horizontal Rule","depth":2},{"id":"emphasis","text":"Emphasis","depth":2},{"id":"bold","text":"Bold","depth":3},{"id":"italics","text":"Italics","depth":3},{"id":"strikethrough","text":"Strikethrough","depth":2},{"id":"links","text":"Links","depth":2},{"id":"autolinks","text":"Autolinks","depth":3},{"id":"inline-links","text":"Inline links","depth":3},{"id":"link-titles","text":"Link titles","depth":3},{"id":"named-anchors","text":"Named Anchors","depth":3},{"id":"chapter-1","text":"Chapter 1","depth":2},{"id":"chapter-2","text":"Chapter 2","depth":2},{"id":"chapter-3-a-namechapter-3a","text":"Chapter 3 <a name=\"chapter-3\"></a>","depth":2},{"id":"images","text":"Images","depth":2},{"id":"blockquotes","text":"Blockquotes","depth":2},{"id":"lists","text":"Lists","depth":2},{"id":"unordered","text":"Unordered","depth":3},{"id":"ordered","text":"Ordered","depth":3},{"id":"time-saving-tip","text":"Time-saving Tip","depth":3},{"id":"todo-list","text":"Todo List","depth":2},{"id":"tables","text":"Tables","depth":2},{"id":"aligning-cells","text":"Aligning cells","depth":3},{"id":"code","text":"Code","depth":2},{"id":"inline-code","text":"Inline code","depth":3},{"id":"fenced-code-block","text":"\"Fenced\" code block","depth":3},{"id":"indented-code","text":"Indented code","depth":3},{"id":"syntax-highlighting","text":"Syntax highlighting","depth":3},{"id":"keyboard-keys","text":"Keyboard Keys","depth":2},{"id":"emojis","text":"Emojis","depth":2},{"id":"front-matter","text":"Front Matter","depth":2},{"id":"yaml","text":"YAML","depth":3},{"id":"toml","text":"TOML","depth":3},{"id":"json","text":"JSON","depth":3},{"id":"math-formulas","text":"Math Formulas","depth":2},{"id":"inline-math-formulas","text":"Inline Math Formulas","depth":3},{"id":"block-math-formulas","text":"Block Math Formulas","depth":3},{"id":"diagrams","text":"Diagrams","depth":2},{"id":"vega-lite-diagram","text":"Vega-lite diagram","depth":2},{"id":"flowchart","text":"Flowchart","depth":2},{"id":"sequence-diagram","text":"Sequence diagram","depth":2},{"id":"flowchart-1","text":"Flowchart","depth":2},{"id":"sequence-diagram-1","text":"Sequence diagram","depth":2},{"id":"gantt-diagram","text":"Gantt diagram","depth":2},{"id":"class-diagram-experimental","text":"Class diagram (experimental)","depth":2},{"id":"plantuml","text":"PlantUML","depth":2},{"id":"raw-html","text":"Raw HTML","depth":2},{"id":"escaping-with-backslashes","text":"Escaping with backslashes","depth":2},{"id":"credits","text":"Credits","depth":2}],"body":"Markdown Syntax Markdown is a easy-to-use markup language for writing and this document contains all supported markdown features. Table of contents Markdown Syntax * Headings * Paragraphs * Breaks * Horizontal Rule * Emphasis + Bold + Italics + Strikethrough * Links + Autolinks + Inline links + Link titles + Named Anchors * Images * Blockquotes * Lists + Unordered + Ordered + Time-saving Tip * Todo List * Tables + Aligning cells * Code + Inline code + \"Fenced\" code block + Indented code + Syntax highlighting * Keyboard Keys * Emojis * Front Matter * Math Formulas + Inline Math Formulas + Block Math Formulas * Diagrams * Raw HTML * Escaping with backslashes * Credits Headings Headings from through are constructed with a for each level: Renders to: h1 Heading h2 Heading h3 Heading h4 Heading h5 Heading h6 Heading Alternatively you can use underlines: H1 ====== H2 Paragraphs Just write normal text: Breaks You can use multiple consecutive newline characters ( ) to create extra spacing between sections in a markdown document. However, if you need to ensure that extra newlines are not collapsed, you can use as many HTML elements as you want. Alternatively you can add **two spaces** spaces at the end of your paragraph to force a soft linebreak. Horizontal Rule The HTML element is for creating a \"thematic break\" between paragraph-level elements. In markdown, you can use of the following for this purpose: : three consecutive underscores : three consecutive dashes : three consecutive asterisks Renders to: Emphasis Bold For emphasizing a snippet of text with a heavier font-weight. The following snippet of text is **rendered as bold text**. renders to: rendered as bold text** Italics For emphasizing a snippet of text with italics. The following snippet of text is _rendered as italicized text_. renders to: rendered as italicized text_ Strikethrough In GFM you can do strickthroughs by wrapping the text with double tildes. Which renders to: ~~Strike through this text.~~ Links Autolinks Autolinks are absolute URIs and email addresses inside and . They are parsed as links, where the URI or email address itself is used as the link's label. Renders to: Images Images have a similar syntax to links but include a preceding exclamation point. or Like links, Images also have a footnote style syntax ![Alt text][id] With a reference later in the document defining the URL location: [id]: \"MarkText logo\" Blockquotes Used for defining a section of quoting text from another source, within your document. To create a blockquote, use before any text you want to quote. Renders to: Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Integer posuere erat a ante. Blockquotes can also be nested: Renders to: Donec massa lacus, ultricies a ullamcorper in, fermentum sed augue. Nunc augue augue, aliquam non hendrerit ac, commodo vel nisi. Sed adipiscing elit vitae augue consectetur a gravida nunc vehicula. Donec auctor odio non est accumsan facilisis. Aliquam id turpis in dolor tinc"},{"slug":"preferences","href":"/docs/preferences","title":"Preferences","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Configuration","hint":"App settings reference","headings":[{"id":"marktext-preferences","text":"MarkText Preferences","depth":2}],"body":"MarkText Preferences Preferences can be controlled and modified in the settings window or via the file in the application data directory. The authoritative list of keys, defaults, and accepted values lives in — the tables below mirror that schema. General Key | Type | Default | Description | ---------------------- | ------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | autoSave | Boolean | | Automatically save the content being edited. | autoSaveDelay | Number | | The delay in milliseconds after a change before a file is saved automatically. Minimum . | titleBarStyle | String | | The title bar style on Linux and Windows: or . | openFilesInNewWindow | Boolean | | Open files in a new window. | openFolderInNewWindow | Boolean | | Open folder via menu in a new window. | zoom | Number | | The zoom level. Between and inclusive. | hideScrollbar | Boolean | | Whether to hide scrollbars. | wordWrapInToc | Boolean | | Whether to enable word wrap in the table of contents. | fileSortBy | String | | Sort files in the opened folder. Optional values: , , . | fileSortOrder | String | | Sort order for files in opened folders: (ascending) or (descending). | startUpAction | String | | The action when MarkText launches. Optional values: , , , . | defaultDirectoryToOpen | String | | The path that should be opened when . | language | String | | The display language MarkText uses. | restoreLayoutState | Boolean | | Restore the previous editor state (open tabs, layout) on startup. | openedFilesInSidebar | Boolean | | Whether to show the *Opened Files* subsection inside the sidebar file tree. | Editor Key | Type | Default | Description | ---------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | editorFontFamily | String | | Editor font family. | fontSize | Number | | Font size in pixels. Range – . | lineHeight | Number | | Line height. Range – . | wrapCodeBlocks | Boolean | | Wrap text inside code blocks. | editorLineWidth | String | | Maximum editor area width. Empty or a value with a , or suffix. | codeFontSize | Number | | Font size inside code blocks. Range – . | codeFontFamily | String | | Code-block font family. | codeBlockLineNumbers | Boolean | | Show line numbers inside code blocks. | trimUnnecessaryCodeBlockEmptyLines | Boolean | | Trim the beginning and ending empty lines in code blocks. | autoPairBracket | Boolean | | Auto-close brackets when editing. | autoPairMarkdownSyntax | Boolean | | Autocomplete markdown syntax. | autoPairQuote | Boolean | | Auto-close quotes. | endOfLine | String | | Newline character at the end of each line: (OS default), , or . | defaultEncoding | String | | The default file encoding. See for the full enum (35 encodings). | autoGuessEncoding | Bool"},{"slug":"key-bindings","href":"/docs/key-bindings","title":"Key bindings","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Configuration","hint":"Default & custom shortcuts","headings":[{"id":"available-modifiers","text":"Available modifiers","depth":2},{"id":"available-keys","text":"Available keys","depth":2},{"id":"available-key-bindings","text":"Available key bindings","depth":2}],"body":"Key Bindings All key bindings can be overwritten with the file. The file is located in the application data directory. Each entry consists of a / pair in JSON format. Here is an example: Available modifiers on macOS on macOS (equal to on macOS) Please don't bind , use instead. Available keys , , and punctuations like or , , , , , , , , , and , , and and Empty string to unset a accelerator Available key bindings Key bindings for macOS Key bindings for Linux Key bindings for Windows"},{"slug":"application-data-directory","href":"/docs/application-data-directory","title":"Application data directory","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Configuration","hint":"Where MarkText stores user data","headings":[],"body":"Application Data Directory The per-user application data directory is located in the following directory: on Windows or on Linux on macOS When portable mode is enabled, the directory location is either the parameter or directory."},{"slug":"environment-variables","href":"/docs/environment-variables","title":"Environment variables","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Configuration","hint":"Runtime environment overrides","headings":[{"id":"development","text":"Development","depth":2}],"body":"Environment Name | Description | ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | | Enable debug mode. | | Print more keyboard information when debug mode is enabled. | | Never show the error dialog to report bugs. | | Overwrite the pandoc path. | Development Name | Description | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ | | Exit on the first error or exception that occurs. | | **Please don't use this!** Used to identify stable releases. |"},{"slug":"cli","href":"/docs/cli","title":"Command line interface","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Configuration","hint":"Flags, switches, exit codes","headings":[],"body":"Command Line Interface should point to your installation of MarkText. The exact location will vary from platform to platform. On macOS, you can create a convenient alias like:"},{"slug":"export","href":"/docs/export","title":"Export a document","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Export & themes","hint":"PDF, HTML, image export","headings":[{"id":"options","text":"Options","depth":2},{"id":"page-options","text":"Page options","depth":3},{"id":"style","text":"Style","depth":3},{"id":"theme","text":"Theme","depth":3},{"id":"header-and-footer","text":"Header and footer","depth":3}],"body":"Export a Document MarkText allows you to export a markdown document as PDF and HTML file or to print the document. Options Page options You can set the page size, orientation and margin before exporting a document. Style Adjust the page style without modify the page theme: Overwrite font family, size and line height. Auto numbering headings. Option to show the front matter on the exported document. Theme MarkText allows you to select a page theme before exporting. You can learn more about page themes here. Header and footer You can include a header and/or footer in the exported document if you choose PDF or printing and also adjust the header/footer style. You can select between no, a single or a three cell header in export options. The header and/or footer appear on each page when defined and the header can be multiline but the footer only single line. Unfortunately, page numbering is currently not supported. An example can be seen below."},{"slug":"themes","href":"/docs/themes","title":"Themes","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Export & themes","hint":"Built-in & custom UI themes","headings":[{"id":"light-themes","text":"Light Themes","depth":2},{"id":"dark-themes","text":"Dark Themes","depth":2},{"id":"switching-themes","text":"Switching Themes","depth":2},{"id":"theme-screenshots","text":"Theme Screenshots","depth":2},{"id":"light-themes-1","text":"Light Themes","depth":3},{"id":"dark-themes-1","text":"Dark Themes","depth":3},{"id":"custom-themes","text":"Custom Themes","depth":2},{"id":"theme-credits","text":"Theme Credits","depth":2}],"body":"Themes MarkText includes 33 built-in themes organized into Light and Dark categories. Each theme provides a complete color scheme for the editor interface and syntax highlighting. The names below match the labels shown in the **Theme** menu. The underlying id stored in under the field can differ — e.g. *Cadmium Light* → , *Cadmium Dark* → , *Graphite Light* → , *Ulysses Light* → . The full label↔id mapping lives in . Light Themes Theme | Description | **Ayu Light** | Warm, comfortable light theme with soft colors | **Cadmium Light** | Clean, minimal light theme (default) | **Catppuccin Latte** | Soothing pastel theme with warm tones | **Everforest Light** | Nature-inspired green-tinted light theme | **Graphite Light** | Cool gray-toned light theme | **Gruvbox Light** | Retro groove with warm, earthy colors | **Rosé Pine Dawn** | Elegant theme with subtle rose accents | **Solarized Light** | Classic precision color scheme | **Tokyo Night Light** | Modern light variant of Tokyo Night | **Ulysses Light** | Inspired by the Ulysses writing app | Dark Themes Theme | Description | **Ayu Dark** | Deep, comfortable dark theme | **Ayu Mirage** | Softer dark variant of Ayu | **Cadmium Dark** | Clean, minimal dark theme | **Catppuccin Mocha** | Rich, warm dark pastel theme | **cyberdream** | Neon-accented futuristic dark theme | **Dracula** | Popular purple-tinted dark theme | **Everforest Dark** | Nature-inspired forest dark theme | **Gruvbox Dark** | Retro groove with warm dark colors | **Horizon Dark** | Vibrant warm dark theme | **Kanagawa** | Inspired by Hokusai's Great Wave painting | **Material Dark** | Google Material Design dark theme | **Monokai Pro** | Professional variant of the classic Monokai | **Nightfox** | Cool-toned dark theme with good contrast | **Nord** | Arctic, bluish clean dark theme | **One Dark** | Atom editor's signature dark theme | **Oxocarbon Dark** | IBM Carbon-inspired minimal dark theme | **Palenight** | Elegant purple-tinted dark theme | **Rosé Pine** | Soho vibes with muted rose tones | **Rosé Pine Moon** | Darker variant of Rosé Pine | **Solarized Dark** | Classic precision dark color scheme | **Synthwave '84** | Retro 80s neon aesthetic | **Tokyo Night** | Modern VSCode-inspired dark theme | **Tokyo Night Storm** | Higher contrast Tokyo Night variant | Switching Themes You can switch themes in several ways: 1. **Menu**: Go to menu and select your preferred theme 2. **Preferences**: Open → tab to preview and select themes 3. **Follow System**: Enable \"Follow System Theme\" to automatically switch between light and dark themes based on your system settings Theme Screenshots Light Themes Cadmium Light | Graphite Light | | | Ulysses Light | Catppuccin Latte | | | Dark Themes Dark | Material Dark | | | One Dark | Dracula | | | Nord | Tokyo Night | | | Custom Themes Custom theme support is planned for a future release. In the meantime, you can use the \"Custom CSS\" option in Preferences → Theme to override theme styles. Theme Cre"},{"slug":"export-themes","href":"/docs/export-themes","title":"Themes for exporting","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Export & themes","hint":"Style your exported HTML","headings":[{"id":"install-a-theme","text":"Install a theme","depth":2},{"id":"create-a-theme","text":"Create a theme","depth":2},{"id":"theme-settings","text":"Theme settings","depth":3}],"body":"Themes (Export) MarkText allows you to modify the appearance of the document that you want to export. By default we provide three themes: Academic, GitHub and Liber (writing theme). Install a theme You can install a theme by copying the file to directory inside the application data directory location but you may need to restart MarkText to detect the theme. Create a theme MarkText use the GitHub theme as basic style that is always available. A custom theme can add additional styles but have to overwrite the GitHub style to make changes such as font family or the underling for heading. You can see all predefined styles here. An example for custom themes can be found here and here. Theme settings A theme currently only have a name ( ) that is defined by a CSS comment at the first line like:"},{"slug":"image-uploader","href":"/docs/image-uploader","title":"Image uploader configuration","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"Export & themes","hint":"Cloud image hosts","headings":[],"body":"Image Uploader Configuration PicGo PicGo is a CLI tool that uploads images to various cloud providers. See the PicGo documentation for installation, configuration, and supported backends. Once PicGo is installed and configured, point MarkText at the PicGo executable in *Preferences → Image* and choose as the *image insert action* (or use it on demand from the image overlay). PicGo is the only built-in uploader; the legacy GitHub uploader was removed in 0.19."},{"slug":"portable","href":"/docs/portable","title":"Portable mode","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"More","hint":"Run MarkText from a USB stick","headings":[{"id":"linux-and-windows","text":"Linux and Windows","depth":2}],"body":"Portable Mode MarkText stores all user configuration inside the application data directory that can be changed with command-line flag. Linux and Windows On Linux and Windows you can also create a directory called to save all user data inside the directory. Like:"},{"slug":"faq","href":"/docs/faq","title":"FAQ","tab":"user","tabLabel":"User docs","group":"More","hint":"Frequently asked questions","headings":[{"id":"what-are-the-supported-platforms","text":"What are the supported platforms?","depth":3},{"id":"is-marktext-open-source-and-free","text":"Is MarkText open-source and free?","depth":3},{"id":"can-i-use-marktext-as-note-managementtaking-app","text":"Can I use MarkText as note management/taking app?","depth":3},{"id":"where-can-i-find-documentation","text":"Where can I find documentation?","depth":3},{"id":"can-i-run-a-portable-version-of-marktext","text":"Can I run a portable version of MarkText?","depth":3},{"id":"how-can-i-report-bugs-and-problems","text":"How can I report bugs and problems","depth":3},{"id":"i-cannot-launch-marktext-on-linux-suid-sandbox","text":"I cannot launch MarkText on Linux (SUID sandbox)","depth":3}],"body":"Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) What are the supported platforms? MarkText is a desktop application and available for: Linux x64 (tested on Debian and Red Hat based distros) macOS 11 (Big Sur) or later, x64 and arm64 (Apple Silicon) Windows 10 or 11, x64 and arm64 Is MarkText open-source and free? Yes, MarkText is licensed under the MIT license and completely free for everyone. The source-code is available on GitHub. Can I use MarkText as note management/taking app? MarkText is a pure markdown editor without feature such as knowledge management and tags but yes, you can do this via the integrated filesystem explorer and task lists. Where can I find documentation? Documentation is currently under development. End-user documentation Developer documentation Can I run a portable version of MarkText? Yes, please see here for further information. How can I report bugs and problems You can report bugs and problems via our GitHub issue tracker. Please provide a detailed description of the problem to better solve the issue. I cannot launch MarkText on Linux (SUID sandbox) _The SUID sandbox helper binary was found, but is not configured correctly._ Normally, you should never get this error but if you disabled user namespaces, this error message may appears in the command output when launching MarkText. To solve the issue, that Chromium cannot start the sandbox (process), you can choose one of the following steps: Enable Linux kernel user namespaces to use the preferred sandbox: . Set correct SUID sandbox helper binary permissions: . This is preferred if you don't want to enable user namespaces. Launch MarkText with argument."},{"slug":"dev/overview","href":"/docs/dev/overview","title":"Developer overview","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Get started","hint":"Where to start contributing","headings":[{"id":"1-project-setup","text":"1. Project Setup","depth":2},{"id":"11-pre-requisites","text":"1.1 Pre-Requisites","depth":3},{"id":"12-linux-specific-pre-requisites","text":"1.2 Linux Specific Pre-requisites","depth":3},{"id":"13-windows-specific-pre-requisites","text":"1.3 Windows Specific Pre-requisites","depth":3},{"id":"14-clone-and-install","text":"1.4 Clone and Install","depth":3},{"id":"15-create-minified-locale-files","text":"1.5 Create minified locale files","depth":3},{"id":"16-run-in-development","text":"1.6 Run in Development","depth":3},{"id":"17-build-for-production","text":"1.7 Build for Production","depth":3},{"id":"2-sub-sections","text":"2. Sub-sections","depth":2}],"body":"Developer Documentation 1. Project Setup 1.1 Pre-Requisites Python ( ) Node.js ( ) — the PR build CI uses Node 22.21.1, the release CI uses Node 24.14.1; any version meeting the minimum should work - Using versions significantly newer than the Electron-bundled Node may cause issues compiling native add-ons A lot of patience 1.2 Linux Specific Pre-requisites Linux environments require additional dependencies, please see Linux Specific Pre-reqs 1.3 Windows Specific Pre-requisites You will need Build Tools for Visual Studio 2022 (Scroll all the way to the bottom) - Additionally, you need **spectre-mitigated MSVC**, go to \"Individual Components\" and select \"MSVC ... - VS2022 C++ Spectre-Mitigated Libs\" - Many native libraries do not support ClangCL well yet, hence we force it to use MSVC in our (pnpm respects this file) 1.4 Clone and Install 1.5 Create minified locale files This is **automatically ran** when building for production, but not for dev for performance 1.6 Run in Development 1.6.1 Some Points to Note: The and processes are **NOT** automatically hot-loaded on edit, you need to **reload the development process** on each edit unfortunately - The good news is Vite bundles it _really really quickly_ so it shouldnt be too big of a hassle Although the process is hot-loaded, loss of states can often lead to **weird errors**. I recommend doing a full reload if this happens Compile targets: - and still compile to - is only (take note when using any legacy libraries) 1.7 Build for Production 2. Sub-sections Performance testing Project architecture Build instructions Debugging Inter-process communication (IPC) Interface Steps to release MarkText Prepare a hotfix TypeScript layout and conventions"},{"slug":"dev/build","href":"/docs/dev/build","title":"Build instructions","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Get started","hint":"Build MarkText from source","headings":[{"id":"container-setup","text":"Container Setup","depth":2},{"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites","depth":3},{"id":"lets-build","text":"Let's build","depth":3},{"id":"important-scripts","text":"Important scripts","depth":3}],"body":"Build Instructions Please see this issue for updates on our efforts to modernize this process!** Clone the repository: Container Setup The easiest way to build Marktext on Linux is from inside a container. Here are the steps for doing so: Below are the complete build instructions, which may help you troubleshoot the above or attempt to build for other platforms. Prerequisites Before you can get started developing, you need set up your build environment: Node.js and pnpm Python for node-gyp C++ compiler and development tools Build is supported on Linux, macOS and Windows Additional development dependencies on Linux:** libX11 (with headers) libxkbfile (with headers) libsecret (with headers) libfontconfig (with headers) rpm (if building on Debian) On Debian-based Linux: On Red Hat-based Linux: On Arch Linux: Additional development dependencies on Windows:** Windows 10 SDK (only needed before Windows 10) Visual Studio 2022 (Build Tools for Visual Studio 2022). You also need the spectre-mitigated MSVC libs — see developer README §1.3 for the exact components to install. Let's build 1. Go to folder 2. Install dependencies: 3. Build MarkText binaries and packages: 4. MarkText binary is located under folder (electron-builder output) Copy the build app to applications folder, or if on Windows run the executable installer. Important scripts Script | Description | ------- | ------------------------------------------------ | | Build MarkText binaries and packages for your OS | | Build and run MarkText in developer mode | | Lint code style | | Run unit tests | For more scripts please see . To improve efficiency during development: 1. Use for development mode. This will automatically reload the window when source code is modified. 2. Use to manually reload the application in development mode if needed. This approach bypasses unnecessary rebuilds and optimizes the developer workflow. However, for CI or release builds, a full rebuild may still be necessary."},{"slug":"dev/linux-dev","href":"/docs/dev/linux-dev","title":"Linux dev environment","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Get started","hint":"Distro-specific tooling notes","headings":[{"id":"system-requirements","text":"System Requirements","depth":2},{"id":"pre-requisites","text":"Pre-requisites","depth":2},{"id":"1-install-system-packages","text":"1. Install System Packages","depth":3},{"id":"2-install-node","text":"2. Install Node","depth":3},{"id":"common-issues--solutions","text":"Common Issues & Solutions","depth":2},{"id":"issue-pnpm-install-fails-with-native-keymap-error","text":"Issue: pnpm install fails with native-keymap error","depth":3},{"id":"issue-electron-fails-to-start-with-libglib-error","text":"Issue: Electron fails to start with libglib error","depth":3},{"id":"issue-permission-errors-during-pnpm-install","text":"Issue: Permission errors during pnpm install","depth":3}],"body":"Linux Specific Pre-requisites These are tested on Ubuntu 24.04.2 LTS and Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (thanks to \"FP Coetzee\") System Requirements Disk space ~1.2G for basic build. RAM: Minimum 4GB recommended (for Electron builds) Pre-requisites Ubuntu Packages: , and 1. Install System Packages for ubuntu for fedora 2. Install Node I recommend using nvm Common Issues & Solutions Issue: pnpm install fails with native-keymap error Error message:** Solution:** This occurs when is missing. Install it: Then retry: Issue: Electron fails to start with libglib error Error message:** Solution:** Install the X11/GUI development libraries: If the issue persists, you may need the full X11 environment: Issue: Permission errors during pnpm install Solution:** Make sure you're not running commands with . pnpm stores packages in a global content-addressable store (default: ). If you encounter permission errors, ensure the store directory is owned by your user:"},{"slug":"dev/debugging","href":"/docs/dev/debugging","title":"Debugging","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Get started","hint":"Attach to main / renderer","headings":[{"id":"use-visual-studio-code","text":"Use Visual Studio Code","depth":2},{"id":"use-chrome-developer-tools","text":"Use Chrome Developer Tools","depth":2},{"id":"debug-built-application","text":"Debug built application","depth":3},{"id":"debug-slow-startup-performance","text":"Debug slow startup performance","depth":2}],"body":"Debugging Use Visual Studio Code The most simplest way is to debug using the configuration. You can set breakpoints and use the statement. Prerequisites:** VS Code's built-in JavaScript debugger (no extension needed — available since VS Code 1.47) Use Chrome Developer Tools You can use the built-in developer tools via in debug mode or connect via using port for the main process and for the renderer process when launched via . Debug built application You can use the default Electron command-line parameters to enable debug mode as described above. Debug slow startup performance Regardless of whether you are using the built or development version, you can use the node-profiler to analysis startup issues. Please follow the tool description for setup. Afterwards, launch the following commands in parallel (e.g. use three terminal windows and launch MarkText last). After the successful launch of MarkText, press on both instances. The tools created two files named and . You can now analyse these files via *Chrome Developer Tools* or *Visual Studio Code*."},{"slug":"dev/architecture","href":"/docs/dev/architecture","title":"Architecture","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Architecture & code","hint":"Process model & module layering","headings":[{"id":"overview","text":"Overview","depth":2},{"id":"introduction-to-marktext","text":"Introduction to MarkText","depth":2},{"id":"application-entry-points","text":"Application entry points","depth":3},{"id":"how-muya-work","text":"How Muya work","depth":3},{"id":"main--and-renderer-process-communication","text":"Main- and renderer process communication","depth":3},{"id":"editor-window-renderer-process","text":"Editor window (renderer process)","depth":3},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","depth":3}],"body":"Project Architecture Overview : Configuration files : Project settings : Compiled source code (main, preload, renderer bundles) : Packaged binaries and installers for distribution : Application assets (themes, icons, etc) included in builds : Documentation and assets : Dependencies : MarkText source code - : Common source files that only require Node.js APIs. Code from this folder can be used in all other folders except . - : Main process source files that require Electron main-process APIs. files can use source code. - : MarkTexts backend that only uses pure JavaScript, BOM and DOM APIs. Don't use Electron or Node.js APIs. - : Frontend that require Electron renderer-process APIs and may use or source code. : Application assets (images, themes, etc) : Contains (unit) tests Introduction to MarkText MarkText is a realtime preview (WYSIWYG) editor for markdown with various markdown extensions and our philosophy is to keep things clean, simple and minimal. The application is built with TypeScript, Vue 3 and CSS on top of Electron (Muya, the editor backend, is currently still JavaScript). We use a few native node libraries and Pinia for renderer state. MarkText can be split in three parts: the core called Muya, the main- and renderer process. Muya provides realtime preview and markdown editing via multiple modules based on a block structure. You can imagine it as the editor backend with modules for markdown parsing, data store as block structure, markdown document transformations according CommonMark and GitHub Flavored Markdown specification with some extra specifications, event listeners and an exporter to generate standalone HTML and markdown files but also to generate the WYSIWYG editor. Muya is single threaded as well as MarkText but use asynchronous functions to boost performance. NOTE: MarkText's source-code editor is provided by CodeMirror and not well optimized nor feature rich. It's not part of Muya and an editor (renderer process) feature that load the markdown text from Muya (export), operate on it and re-import the text into Muya when switching to preview mode. NOTE: Muya requires a core refactoring to provide better modularization, APIs and plugins. Furthermore, the data structure need improvements for better performance and stability. The editor represents the view and is split into two parts. The first is the main process that have full access to Electron and all OS features. It's mainly used for IO, user interaction with native dialogs and controlls the editor windows. The main process should not (be long) blocked by synchronous operations. The renderer process is the real editor and also a host for Muya. It's responsible for all graphical elements ( ), data ( ) and data synchronization. A renderer process is spawned for each window, operates on its own and is controlled by the main process. It contains two text editors: the realtime preview editor provided by Muya and the source-code one by CodeMirror with special features such as ta"},{"slug":"dev/interface","href":"/docs/dev/interface","title":"Interface","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Architecture & code","hint":"Muya & renderer public interfaces","headings":[{"id":"main-interface","text":"Main interface","depth":2},{"id":"titlebar","text":"Titlebar","depth":3},{"id":"sidebar","text":"Sidebar","depth":3},{"id":"editor","text":"Editor","depth":3}],"body":"Interface Main interface Green: titlebar Orange: sidebar Red: editor with tabs and per-tab notification at the bottom Titlebar The titlebar is located at the top of the window and shows the current opened file path and the menu on Linux and Windows. On macOS we're using client-side decorations (CSD) that look similar to the picture above. On Linux and Windows there are two type of titlebar: a custom CSD that you can see in the image above and the native one. Sidebar The sidebar is an optional feature of MarkText that contains three panels and has a variable width. The first panel is a tree view of the opened root directory; it also hosts a collapsible *Opened Files* subsection (toggle via the preference). The latter two panels are a folder searcher (find in files) that is powered by ripgrep and a table of contents of the currently opened document. Editor The editor is the core element that hosts the realtime preview editor called Muya and consists of three parts. Tabs are located at the top and at the bottom the per-tab notification bar is located for events like file changed or deleted. The main part is the editor that is either provided by Muya or CodeMirror for the source-code editor. There are multiple overlays available like inline toolbar, emoji picker, quick insert or image tools."},{"slug":"dev/ipc","href":"/docs/dev/ipc","title":"IPC","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Architecture & code","hint":"Typed mt:: channel catalog","headings":[{"id":"channel-naming","text":"Channel naming","depth":2},{"id":"the-four-channel-categories","text":"The four channel categories","depth":2},{"id":"renderer-side","text":"Renderer side","depth":2},{"id":"main-side","text":"Main side","depth":2},{"id":"adding-a-new-channel","text":"Adding a new channel","depth":2}],"body":"Inter-Process Communication (IPC) The renderer runs sandboxed ( , , — see ), so it can't import directly. All renderer↔main traffic goes through the typed preload bridge exposed in as and a few sibling globals ( , , , etc.). Channel names are typed by the contract in — wrong channel, wrong arg arity, or wrong return shape all fail at . See TYPESCRIPT.md for the TypeScript-side details. Channel naming Renderer↔main channels are prefixed with (e.g. , ). A small number of legacy internal channels don't follow this convention (e.g. ); new channels should always use . The four channel categories defines four interfaces: Interface | Direction | Semantics | ---------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------ | | renderer → main | round-trip ( ) | | renderer → main | fire-and-forget ( ) | | renderer → main | synchronous reply ( ) | | main → renderer | push event ( / on) | Each entry tells you the args tuple and (for invoke/sync) the return type: Renderer side Use the global (the typed wrapper exposed by the preload bridge). Don't — it isn't available under sandboxing. and follow the same shape. For filesystem and path helpers, prefer the typed convenience APIs already exposed via , , , etc. — they wrap the underlying / channels and keep call sites short. Main side Channels are wired with (invoke), (send / sync), or (push): Adding a new channel 1. Add an entry to the appropriate interface in (pick invoke / send / sync / main-event based on the direction and shape). 2. Wire the main-process handler in (or the relevant feature module). 3. Call it from the renderer through or, if it deserves a dedicated facade, expose a method on one of the typed bridges in . After step 1, flags every existing call site that doesn't match the new shape — use that as your migration checklist."},{"slug":"dev/typescript","href":"/docs/dev/typescript","title":"TypeScript","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Architecture & code","hint":"TS conventions & strict mode","headings":[{"id":"tsconfig-layout","text":"tsconfig layout","depth":2},{"id":"path-aliases","text":"Path aliases","depth":2},{"id":"where-types-live","text":"Where types live","depth":2},{"id":"ipc-contract","text":"IPC contract","depth":2},{"id":"muya-boundary","text":"muya boundary","depth":2},{"id":"typedemitter","text":"TypedEmitter","depth":2},{"id":"pinia-stores","text":"Pinia stores","depth":2},{"id":"strict-mode-landmines","text":"Strict-mode landmines","depth":2},{"id":"deferred-work","text":"Deferred work","depth":2},{"id":"type-checking","text":"Type-checking","depth":2}],"body":"TypeScript MarkText is a TypeScript project. Every file under (except ), the build scripts under , the test specs under , the build config ( ), and the test configs ( , ) are TS. The only JavaScript that ships in the source tree is — the legacy editor engine, which will be replaced by the upstream TS muya at The migration's ambient declaration is the bridge; consumers always go through that file, never the underlying . tsconfig layout Single root project, no / : Why a single project: nothing under needs declaration emission (the bundler is electron-vite), and the renderer, preload and main processes share enough types that splitting them into project references would mostly add ceremony. A single project keeps the wiring minimal without giving up strict mode. Relevant settings ( ): (every strict flag on) — too disruptive given existing index-access patterns (tabs, listToc) — kept off to keep the buffered-state restore path (which carries optional fields through JSON serialization) tolerant of ≡ \"key not present\" — every directory under is now except , which the rest of the tree reaches through the ambient declarations in — vue-tsc only type-checks; electron-vite handles the actual bundling Path aliases Defined in both and (the two must stay in sync): Alias | Maps to | | | | | | (legacy) | | | carries the same aliases plus → for the few unit specs that reach into main-process code. Where types live ** ** — cross-process types (IPC contract, file/tab shapes, preferences, menu, bus, TypedEmitter helper). Pure type artefacts, no runtime. Importable from any process via . ** ** — ambient declarations ( , , , ). only; no runtime. **Co-located** — domain types specific to one feature live next to the code (e.g. defines its own ). IPC contract The single source of truth is . Four channel maps: — renderer → main, returns Promise — renderer → main, fire-and-forget — synchronous renderer → main — main → renderer push events The preload bridge ( ) consumes these as generics: Every renderer-side call is type-checked: wrong channel name, wrong arg arity, wrong arg types, all surface at compile time. To add a new channel: 1. Add an entry to the appropriate interface in . 2. Wire the handler in via / . 3. Use it from the renderer via . muya boundary stays JavaScript. The ambient declaration covers the ~21 import paths the rest of the codebase actually uses ( , , , the dozen-plus overlay components, etc.). Most entries are -typed shims — good enough for the consumer side, and they delete cleanly the day upstream TS muya lands. TypedEmitter Main-process classes that historically extended now extend from . Event names + listener-argument tuples are typed: Wrong event names or mismatched listener arities fail at compile time. Pinia stores All 9 active Pinia stores ( ) are typed. Seven are Setup Stores (`defineStore('id', () => { ... return { ...refs, ...computeds, ...actions } }) editor.ts preferences.ts` remain Options Stores because Pinia's Options-Store typing is"},{"slug":"dev/performance","href":"/docs/dev/performance","title":"Performance","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Architecture & code","hint":"Measurement workflow","headings":[{"id":"1-testing-main-process","text":"1. Testing main process","depth":2},{"id":"11-navigate-to-chrome-inspect","text":"1.1 Navigate to Chrome Inspect","depth":2},{"id":"12-record-performance","text":"1.2 Record Performance","depth":2},{"id":"13-alternative-inspect","text":"1.3 Alternative: inspect","depth":2}],"body":"Performance Testing We mainly rely on Chrome's tab to analyse and record the performance of MarkText Electron is split into 3 processes: , and . We will be focusing on and since these are the heaviest 1. Testing process This launches a **production build** of Marktext that has a break-point **before** the first line of Javascript is ran A debugger at port is also attached 1.1 Navigate to Chrome Inspect Go to Press , and add Press \"Inspect\" on the entry that appears below (might take a while) 1.2 Record Performance When Developers Tools first launches, you will notice the breakpoint that is set Simply go to , press , and wait for MarkText to launch fully, then Stop the recording 1.3 Alternative: If you do not need the breakpoint to test start-up performance, simply run: 2. Testing This previews the most recent output via with (so it behaves like a production launch). It does **not** rebuild — re-run first if your sources changed. Press to open Dev Tools and press to benchmark start-up render performance"},{"slug":"dev/release","href":"/docs/dev/release","title":"Release process","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Release","hint":"Cut a public release","headings":[{"id":"prerequisites","text":"Prerequisites","depth":2},{"id":"1-cut-a-release-branch-first-rc-only","text":"1. Cut a release branch (first RC only)","depth":2},{"id":"2-bump-packagejson","text":"2. Bump package.json","depth":2},{"id":"3-commit-and-push-the-branch","text":"3. Commit and push the branch","depth":2},{"id":"4-tag-and-push","text":"4. Tag and push","depth":2},{"id":"5-open-a-tracking-pr-rc-only","text":"5. Open a tracking PR (RC only)","depth":2},{"id":"6-monitor-the-workflow","text":"6. Monitor the workflow","depth":2},{"id":"7-verify-the-published-release","text":"7. Verify the published release","depth":2},{"id":"8-post-stable-cleanup-after-stable-vxy0-ships","text":"8. Post-stable cleanup (after stable vX.Y.0 ships)","depth":2}],"body":"Releasing MarkText The release pipeline is triggered by pushing a tag. The workflow ( ) then runs **validate → build (4-platform matrix) → publish** and creates a GitHub Release with installers and . The flow below covers both release candidates and stable releases — same steps, only the version string differs. Prerequisites Push access to CLI authenticated ( ) A clean checkout of the latest 1. Cut a release branch (first RC only) Reuse the same branch for every RC of that minor version ( , , …) **and** the eventual stable tag. For follow-ups, just and skip to step 2. 2. Bump Edit the field — it is the only file you need to change. Stage | Version string | Release candidate | , , … | Stable | | 3. Commit and push the branch 4. Tag and push A in the tag (e.g. ) tells the workflow to mark the GitHub Release as **pre-release** automatically. Plain tags publish as stable releases. 5. Open a tracking PR (RC only) Open a **draft** PR from → for visibility. Do **not** merge it until the matching stable tag is pushed — merging an RC commit would freeze at the RC version. 6. Monitor the workflow Approximate timing: validate ~30 s · build matrix ~15–30 min (4 platforms in parallel) · publish ~1 min. 7. Verify the published release Confirm: badge on the release page (RC only) **24 assets**: - **Linux** (5): , , , , - **macOS arm64** (4): , , , - **macOS x64** (4): , , , - **Windows x64** (3): , , - **Windows arm64** (3): , , - **Auto-updater metadata** (4): , , , - **Checksums** (1): Auto-generated release notes list the PRs merged since the previous tag 8. Post-stable cleanup (after stable ships) 1. Mark the tracking PR from step 5 ready for review and merge into 2. Open a follow-up PR bumping 's to the next dev version (e.g. ) For hotfixes off a previously-released tag, see RELEASE_HOTFIX.md. Once the hotfix branch is ready, steps 2–7 above apply."},{"slug":"dev/hotfix","href":"/docs/dev/hotfix","title":"Hotfix release","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Release","hint":"Patch a published release","headings":[],"body":"Prepare a hotfix Create a hotfix branch: Make changes to the code and/or changes from another branch and commit changes. Test the hotfix and binaries. Release a new MarkText version. How to cherry pick?** You can pick commits from another branch and apply the commit to the current one. Please resolve all conflicts and the changes if needed."},{"slug":"dev/code-of-conduct","href":"/docs/dev/code-of-conduct","title":"Code of conduct","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Project","hint":"Community guidelines","headings":[{"id":"our-pledge","text":"Our Pledge","depth":2},{"id":"our-standards","text":"Our Standards","depth":2},{"id":"our-responsibilities","text":"Our Responsibilities","depth":2},{"id":"scope","text":"Scope","depth":2},{"id":"enforcement","text":"Enforcement","depth":2},{"id":"attribution","text":"Attribution","depth":2}],"body":"Contributor Covenant Code of Conduct Our Pledge In the interest of fostering an open and welcoming environment, we as contributors and maintainers pledge to making participation in our project and our community a harassment-free experience for everyone, regardless of age, body size, disability, ethnicity, gender identity and expression, level of experience, education, socio-economic status, nationality, personal appearance, race, religion, or sexual identity and orientation. Our Standards Examples of behavior that contributes to creating a positive environment include: Using welcoming and inclusive language Being respectful of differing viewpoints and experiences Gracefully accepting constructive criticism Focusing on what is best for the community Showing empathy towards other community members Examples of unacceptable behavior by participants include: The use of sexualized language or imagery and unwelcome sexual attention or advances Trolling, insulting/derogatory comments, and personal or political attacks Public or private harassment Publishing others' private information, such as a physical or electronic address, without explicit permission Other conduct which could reasonably be considered inappropriate in a professional setting Our Responsibilities Project maintainers are responsible for clarifying the standards of acceptable behavior and are expected to take appropriate and fair corrective action in response to any instances of unacceptable behavior. Project maintainers have the right and responsibility to remove, edit, or reject comments, commits, code, wiki edits, issues, and other contributions that are not aligned to this Code of Conduct, or to ban temporarily or permanently any contributor for other behaviors that they deem inappropriate, threatening, offensive, or harmful. Scope This Code of Conduct applies both within project spaces and in public spaces when an individual is representing the project or its community. Examples of representing a project or community include using an official project e-mail address, posting via an official social media account, or acting as an appointed representative at an online or offline event. Representation of a project may be further defined and clarified by project maintainers. Enforcement Instances of abusive, harassing, or otherwise unacceptable behavior may be reported by contacting the project team at ransixi@gmail.com. All complaints will be reviewed and investigated and will result in a response that is deemed necessary and appropriate to the circumstances. The project team is obligated to maintain confidentiality with regard to the reporter of an incident. Further details of specific enforcement policies may be posted separately. Project maintainers who do not follow or enforce the Code of Conduct in good faith may face temporary or permanent repercussions as determined by other members of the project's leadership. Attribution This Code of Conduct is adapted from the [Contributor Coven"},{"slug":"changelog","href":"/docs/changelog","title":"Changelog","tab":"dev","tabLabel":"Developer docs","group":"Project","hint":"Release history","headings":[{"id":"0171","text":"0.17.1","depth":2},{"id":"0170","text":"0.17.0","depth":2},{"id":"0163","text":"0.16.3","depth":2},{"id":"0162","text":"0.16.2","depth":2},{"id":"0161","text":"0.16.1","depth":2},{"id":"0160","text":"0.16.0","depth":2},{"id":"0151","text":"0.15.1","depth":2},{"id":"0150","text":"0.15.0","depth":2},{"id":"0140","text":"0.14.0","depth":3},{"id":"01365","text":"0.13.65","depth":3},{"id":"01350","text":"0.13.50","depth":3},{"id":"01225","text":"0.12.25","depth":3},{"id":"01220","text":"0.12.20","depth":3},{"id":"01142","text":"0.11.42","depth":3},{"id":"01021","text":"0.10.21","depth":3},{"id":"0925","text":"0.9.25","depth":3},{"id":"0812","text":"0.8.12","depth":3},{"id":"0717","text":"0.7.17","depth":3},{"id":"0614","text":"0.6.14","depth":3},{"id":"052","text":"0.5.2","depth":3},{"id":"040","text":"0.4.0","depth":3},{"id":"030","text":"0.3.0","depth":3}],"body":"0.17.1 Added experimental native support for Apple M1 (see #2983 if you have issues). :butterfly:Optimization** Improved behavior of inline markdown character auto pairing. :beetle:Bug fix** Fixed crash at startup when a key binding was unset. Fixed paste handler for images. Fixed deletion behavior of a selected image. 0.17.0 Mark Text is now MarkText! We'd like to thank all contributors and users that have been using MarkText and worked on this release. :warning:Breaking Changes:** Changed multiple default key bindings. Changed key binding handling: all key bindings are now mapped to the corresponding US key bindings. E.g. on a German keyboard will now produce because is equivalent to . Image uploader: SM.MS was removed, GitHub uploader is deprecated and will be replaced by PicGo in our next release. :cactus:Feature** Added support for image path variables. Added command-line image uploader and reworked settings. Added regular expression group replacement to searcher. Added PlantUML diagram support. Added support for chemical equations in math mode. Added automatic call to search for find-in-file when the pane is opened. Open local non-markdown files in default application. Added support to use all menu entries for key binding. Added window zoom via default mouse event. Zoom level is now restored on startup. Added PicGo to upload images. Ability to invalidate image cache on demand. Added settings page to configure key bindings. :butterfly:Optimization** Improved inline markdown autocomplete support. Improved preference design. Relative image paths are now converted to forward slashes on Windows for better compatibility. Center sidebar image when no search results are available. Updated emoji database. Improved sidebar file handling UX by providing more error messages. :beetle:Bug fix** Fixed an issue with code block cursor positioning. Fixed invalid mermaid diagram representation when exporting as PDF. Fixed rendering issue with LaTeX formulas Fixed an issue that may caused editor flickering while pressing . Fixed Table of Contents sidebar display issue. Fixed editor overflow when the sidbar is open. Fixed a crash that occurred due to a bug in the markdown footnote parser. Fixed issue with shortcuts that included . Fixed multiple issues on non-US keyboards. Normalized source-code editor cursor. Fixed preference scaling issue and added missing scrollbar. Fixed a rendering issue when multiple elements were selected via keyboard. Fixed preference migrations were always executed on start-up. Fixed scrolling and search match positing with high DPI scaling. Fixed slow startup on Windows due to a blocking call. Fixed an issue that caused the paste handler to insert only HTML content because if HTML rendering was disabled. Fixed file rename with editor dialog. Fixed URL open handler when clicking a link in the editor. Fixed an issue that prevented to unindent a list item. Fixed multiple XSS issues on language selector and data input. Fixed a bug that HT"}]
